M1: What dimensions do the MQ metrics have?
fetch metric.series, from:-2h
| filter metric.key == "ibmmq.queue_manager.availability" or metric.key == "ibmmq.queue.depth"
| limit 10


Check: expand one record and look at the dimension list. We're looking for queue_manager, queue_name, a dt.entity.* ID, a host, or (best case) anything that carries an APMID or app name.

M2: Do queue manager entities carry app tags?
fetch `dt.entity.ibmmq:queue_manager`
| fields id, name = entity.name, tags
| limit 30


If you get an error that the entity type doesn't exist, run this to find the correct name:

fetch dt.system.data_objects
| filter contains(lower(name), "mq")
| fields name


Check: whether any queue manager has APMID: / Portfolio:Claims tags for a Claims app, rather than only APP00005707.

M3: Do queue names identify the app?
timeseries d = avg(ibmmq.queue.depth), by:{queue_manager, queue_name}, from:-30m
| fields queue_manager, queue_name
| filter not(startsWith(queue_name, "SYSTEM."))
| sort queue_manager asc, queue_name asc
| limit 200


Check: whether queue names contain an APMID or app name (e.g. CLAIMS.ORCH.REQ, APP00236568.IN), or whether each queue manager is dedicated to one app (e.g. a QM name containing CLM/BCBD).

M4: How do MQ problems look in Davis?
fetch dt.davis.problems, from:-30d
| sort timestamp desc
| dedup display_id
| filter contains(lower(event.name), "mq") or contains(toString(affected_entity_types), "ibmmq")
| fields display_id, event.status, event.name, affected_entity_ids,
         affected_entity_types, entity_tags
| limit 20
